title: 'C-c s s : skg-set-repo'
pid: cdea5df7-2eb4-4f7a-b7a1-47abf849bc89
body: |2-
  Changes the skgrepo of the node at point.
  The prompt starts with the current skgrepo as editable minibuffer text.
  You can type the new skgrepo name directly,
  or use shift-left / shift-right to cycle through owned skgrepos.
  Press C-? from the prompt to open an org buffer listing every
  configured skgrepo name as a headline,
  with that skgrepo's path as the headline body.

  The command only edits the node's metadata in the current buffer.
  Save the view afterward to apply the move.

  A write-protected occurrence is not changed -- the save would
  refuse its skgrepo edit -- and produces a loud WARNING,
  with the full ID list written to =*Messages*=.

  What the move does to the relationships touching the moved nodes, the
  save decides, whether or not the view shows them. One it would leave
  more public than a node's new home (an inbound relationship of a move
  to a more private skgrepo) is raised to that home, and the save's
  warnings say so. If it would leave others at their old, more private
  skgrepos, it saves nothing yet: it lists them in
  =*SKG Stranded Relationships*= and asks whether to reset all of
  them to their new defaults, none, or each in turn; then it saves with
  that answer, or not at all if you quit.
